From c370baa328022cbd46c59c821d1b467a97f047be Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Qiuxu Zhuo <qiuxu.zhuo@intel.com>
Date: Fri, 24 Dec 2021 04:11:26 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] EDAC/i10nm: Release mdev/mbase when failing to detect HBM

On systems without HBM (High Bandwidth Memory) mdev/mbase are not
released/unmapped.

Add the code to release mdev/mbase when failing to detect HBM.

[Tony: re-word commit message]

Cc: <stable@vger.kernel.org>
Fixes: c945088384d0 ("EDAC/i10nm: Add support for high bandwidth memory")
Reported-by: kernel test robot <lkp@intel.com>
Reported-by: Dan Carpenter <dan.carpenter@oracle.com>
Signed-off-by: Qiuxu Zhuo <qiuxu.zhuo@intel.com>
Signed-off-by: Tony Luck <tony.luck@intel.com>
Link: https://lore.kernel.org/r/20211224091126.1246-1-qiuxu.zhuo@intel.com

diff --git a/drivers/edac/i10nm_base.c b/drivers/edac/i10nm_base.c
index 83345bfac246..6cf50ee0b77c 100644
--- a/drivers/edac/i10nm_base.c
+++ b/drivers/edac/i10nm_base.c
@@ -358,6 +358,9 @@ static int i10nm_get_hbm_munits(void)

 			mbase = ioremap(base + off, I10NM_HBM_IMC_MMIO_SIZE);
 			if (!mbase) {
+				pci_dev_put(d->imc[lmc].mdev);
+				d->imc[lmc].mdev = NULL;
+
 				i10nm_printk(KERN_ERR, "Failed to ioremap for hbm mc 0x%llx\n",
 					     base + off);
 				return -ENOMEM;
@@ -368,6 +371,12 @@ static int i10nm_get_hbm_munits(void)

 			mcmtr = I10NM_GET_MCMTR(&d->imc[lmc], 0);
 			if (!I10NM_IS_HBM_IMC(mcmtr)) {
+				iounmap(d->imc[lmc].mbase);
+				d->imc[lmc].mbase = NULL;
+				d->imc[lmc].hbm_mc = false;
+				pci_dev_put(d->imc[lmc].mdev);
+				d->imc[lmc].mdev = NULL;
+
 				i10nm_printk(KERN_ERR, "This isn't an hbm mc!\n");
 				return -ENODEV;
 			}
-- 
2.25.1

### Modifications in Each File

**File: `drivers/edac/i10nm_base.c`**
1. **Memory Device Release on Failure to Map:**
   - When `ioremap` fails (indicated by `if (!mbase)`), the code now releases the memory device (`mdev`) associated with the memory controller, nullifying the pointer afterward. This prevents memory leaks when there is no High Bandwidth Memory (HBM).
   ```c
   pci_dev_put(d->imc[lmc].mdev);
   d->imc[lmc].mdev = NULL;
   ```

2. **Memory Unmapping and Device Release on Invalid HBM:**
   - If it is determined that the memory controller is not HBM (indicated by `if (!I10NM_IS_HBM_IMC(mcmtr))`), the existing base memory mapping (`mbase`) is unmapped, the related memory device is released, and the pointer is set to `NULL`. This ensures that system resources are properly managed.
   ```c
   iounmap(d->imc[lmc].mbase);
   d->imc[lmc].mbase = NULL;
   d->imc[lmc].hbm_mc = false;
   pci_dev_put(d->imc[lmc].mdev);
   d->imc[lmc].mdev = NULL;
   ```

### Relationships Between Modifications
The modifications are closely related in terms of resource management. Both code segments aim to ensure that resources allocated for memory devices (`mdev`) and their corresponding memory bases (`mbase`) are properly released and reset to `NULL` in scenarios where HBM detection fails, either due to a mapping failure or an invalid memory controller type. This proactive handling prevents potential memory leaks or misuse of invalid pointers during runtime, maintaining the stability and integrity of the kernel's memory management system.

        You will be given the root cause of a bug, the commit message and the corresponding patch.
        You need to extract all code statements that lead to the bug from the patch, based on the root cause and the commit message.

        Root cause:
        The bug arises from the failure to release and unmap the memory device (`mdev`) and its associated base memory (`mbase`) when the system does not detect High Bandwidth Memory (HBM). This oversight can lead to memory leaks and potential misuse of resources, as the allocated memory remains unfreed, causing instability in the kernel's memory management.

